    "_meanings": [
        ".²1 An inspired poet or sage, a singer of sacred hymns, ({%<ab>e. g.</ab>%} {#kutsa#},\n {#vasizWa, atri, agastya#} <ab>&c.</ab>). [These Ṛṣis form a class of beings distinct\n from gods, men, Asuras <ab>&c.</ab> (<ls>Av. 10. 10. 26</ls>). They are the authors or {%seers%}\n of the Vedic hymns; {#fzayo mantradrazwAro vasizWAdayaH#}; or, according\n to Yāska, {#yasya vAkyaM sa fziH#}, {%<ab>i. e.</ab>%} they are the persons to\n whom the Vedic hymns were revealed. In every Sūkta the {#fzi#} is mentioned\n along with the {#devatA, Candas#} and {#viniyoga#}. The later works mention\n seven Ṛṣis or {%saptarṣis%} whose names, according to <ls>Śat. Br.</ls>, are\n {#gOtama, BaradvAja, viSvAmitra, jamadagni, vasizWa, kaSyapa#} and {#atri#};\n according to Mahābhārata, {#marIci, atri, aNgiras, pulaha, kratu, pulastya#}\n and {#vasizWa#}; Manu calls these sages Prajāpatis or progenitors of\n mankind, and gives ten names, three more being added to the latter list,\n {%<ab>i. e.</ab>%} {#dakza#} or {#pracetas, Bfgu#} and {#nArada#}. In astronomy\n the seven Ṛṣis form the constellation of “the Great Bear”; {#yatrA#}\n {#sapta fzIn para ekamAhuH#} <ls>Rv. 10. 82. 2.</ls>]\n",
        ".²2 A sanctified sage, saint, an ascetic, anchorite; (there are usually\n three classes of these saints); {#devarzi, brahmarzi#} and {#rAjArzi#};\n sometimes four more are added; {#maharzi, paramarzi, Srutarzi#} and {#kARqarzi#}.\n",
        ".²3 A ray of light.\n",
        ".²4 An imaginary circle.\n",
        ".²5 A hymn ({#mantra#}) composed by a Ṛṣi; {#etadvo'stu tapoyuktaM dadAmItyfzicoditam#}\n <ls>Mb. 12. 11. 18</ls>;\n",
        ".²6 The Veda; <ls>P. III. 2. 186</ls>.\n",
        ".²7 A symbolical expression for number seven.\n",
        ".²8 Life; <ls>Bhāg. 10. 87. 50</ls>.\n",
        ".²9 The moon.\n"
    ],
